explanation:
## One pass, and the step over the space

The shape that works without a container:

```cpp
std::size_t i{ 0 };
while (i < line.size())
{
    std::size_t j{ i };
    while (j < line.size() && line[j] != ' ') ++j;

    // [i, j) is one word
    i = j + 1;          // step over the space, not onto it
}
```

The outer loop's position always sits at the first character of a word. The inner loop runs to the space after it — or to the end of the line, which is why the guard is `j < size()` first and `line[j] != ' '` second. Then `i = j + 1`, which steps **over** the separator. `i = j` is the bug that gives an infinite loop on a line with a space in it, and it is the one most first attempts have.

When `j` reached the end of the line, `i = j + 1` is one past the end, the outer condition fails, and the loop stops — so the last word, which has no space after it, needs no special case. That is the whole reason to walk the string rather than to split it.

## The version that splits

In Python the split version is obviously right and obviously shorter:

```python
pieces = line.split(" ")
return (len(pieces), max(len(w) for w in pieces))
```

and it is worth knowing why it is correct here and would not be in general. `split(" ")` with an explicit separator splits on every single space, so `"a  b"` yields `["a", "", "b"]` — an empty word. The problem guarantees single spaces, so that cannot arise. `line.split()` with no argument collapses runs of whitespace instead, which would be the right call if the input were not guaranteed, and would quietly disagree with the C++ version if it were written to the letter of the loop above.

That disagreement between two correct-looking programs is the interesting part of this exercise: the guarantees in the statement are what make the two implementations equivalent, and the moment a guarantee is dropped they stop being.

## Getting the output right

Two numbers, one space, one newline. `std::cout << a << ' ' << b << '\n'` and `print(a, b)` both produce exactly that. A trailing space before the newline would be forgiven by the judge, which forgives trailing whitespace per line, but a comma or two lines would not be.
